{"name":"Twine","hex":"#c19156","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Twine","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Twine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Twine","blocks":[{"text":"Twine is a strong thread, light string or cord composed of string in which two or more thinner strands are twisted, and then twisted together (plied). The strands are plied in the opposite direction to that of their twist, which adds torsional strength to the cord and keeps it from unravelling. This process is sometimes called reverse wrap. The same technique used for making twine is also used to make thread, which is thinner, yarn, and rope, which is stronger and thicker, generally with three or more strands."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Multiple_thread.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twine","wikidata":"Q762984"},{"title":"Twine (software)","blocks":[{"text":"Twine is a free open-source tool created by Chris Klimas for making interactive fiction and hypertext fiction in the form of web pages. It is available on macOS, Windows, and Linux."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Twine_vector_logo.svg/330px-Twine_vector_logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twine_(software)","wikidata":"Q15411624"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Making_Twine_%281915_by_Elstner_Hilton%29.jpg/500px-Making_Twine_%281915_by_Elstner_Hilton%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Making_Twine_%281915_by_Elstner_Hilton%29.jpg/1280px-Making_Twine_%281915_by_Elstner_Hilton%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"ht